From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] viogpu3d: video memory, a GPU memory limit and timeout recovery

- Dedicated video memory: with a host-visible memory window, a second, local memory segment over it (WDDM 2.0),
  which DXGI reports as dedicated video memory. Programs pick GPUs by it (Topaz Video AI took a GPU with none for
  unusable and ran on the CPU). No allocation is placed there, and it is not CPU-visible: most of the window has
  nothing mapped until Venus maps blobs, and Windows touching it hung every Direct3D program. The system memory
  segment GpuMmu adds moves to 3 (VioGpuAdapter::SysmemSegmentId).
- A GPU memory limit per VM: every resource is charged by its size (computed from its description: textures the CPU
  never maps have no guest memory but live on the host) against GpuMemoryLimitMB (driver key, default 8192, 512 to
  65536); over it CreateAllocation answers STATUS_NO_MEMORY, as a full graphics card does. Without it one program
  creating resources without end (Topaz Video AI's upgrade pop-up keeps a texture per animation frame) takes the
  host's memory until its kernel kills the GPU process.
- GPU timeout recovery: DxgkDdiResetFromTimeout was a stub and the INF turned timeouts off (TdrDebugMode=1), so a
  hung host froze the VM for good. The commander now numbers its work by generation: a reset starts a new one,
  older work is abandoned (never sent on, never reported complete, its allocations no longer busy), late host
  replies included; running commands are counted under the lock the reset takes. The INF sets TdrDebugMode=2 and
  TdrDelay=10 (host-side work, shader compiles and large uploads, can take seconds). Tested by freezing the GPU
  process on the host under a rendering program: 15 and 25 s freezes recover with one and two resets.
---
